WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:04.150
Today I just want to
show people how to get

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.150
started, making
a basic Shiny app

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.670
and just understanding
the basic concepts.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:12.080
In order to do that, we can
just start from the website.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.880
This is the Shiny
for Python website.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.250
It's shiny.rstudio.com/py.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.440
I know that might be really
little in the browser,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.080
but we'll post a link for that.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.620
And the easiest way to get
started just working on code

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.510
is just click on examples.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:33.370
And that will take you to
this website, shinylive.io,

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.970
where you can just
work on your code here.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.070
You can look at the
examples, edit the code,

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.090
and run the apps.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.010
And this is one of the
most exciting things,

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.390
I think, is that
you can use Shiny

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:47.860
without having to
install anything,

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.020
without having to install
even Python on your system.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.760
You can just get in here and
start working right away.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.310
And then as you
develop an app that

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:04.150
becomes more sophisticated, you
can work in a regular Python

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.770
installation, but
to get started,

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.080
this is a great way to do it.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.940
So let's take a look
at this example app.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.190
There's a whole bunch of
different examples over here,

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.910
but by default, it just
starts on this page.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.390
And to understand how a
Shiny application is built,

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.170
there's two main components.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.900
There's the ui, which
is the part that's

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:23.960
displayed in the web browser.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.877
This is a web page that's
displayed in the web

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:30.370
browser over here, and then
there's the server logic,

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:34.750
and this is Python code that
gets executed when somebody

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.890
connects to this application.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.290
It executes this code
once, and then it

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:45.370
will re-execute reactive
functions in response

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.770
to user input.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:52.540
So there's a ui here,
there's the server code here,

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.470
and then that stuff gets put
together into a Shiny app

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:57.770
object over here.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:01.930
And so we're calling
this app object App.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.630
That's what you have to call
it for this all to work.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:05.990
Let's take a look at the ui.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:11.720
So in this case, we
have an input slider,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.160
which is this thing over here,
and then output_text_verbatim.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.150
So this is a text
output, and the verbatim

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.870
means that it's printed as code
with a monospace font instead

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:26.830
of being printed as
normal HTML text.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:35.170
And as we change this slider,
it causes this output to change.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.440
All right, so we've got
this input and this output

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.930
component, and they're
wrapped up inside of something

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:41.890
called a page fluid.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.390
Now, we don't have to know the
details about this right now,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.620
but for most applications
at this point,

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:52.538
we can just wrap these
components in a page fluid.

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:54.580
All right, so now what's
connecting them together

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.920
is this server logic here.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:57.850
This is a function.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.200
It's called server.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.050
And as I said earlier,
it gets executed once

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.030
when the user connects
to the application.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:07.771
Now, there's a bunch of
these decorators here.

00:03:07.771 --> 00:03:10.900
This is saying we've got
this thing that's an output,

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.200
it's rendering text,
and then here we have--

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:20.678
we're defining the function
txt, which returns a string.

00:03:20.678 --> 00:03:22.720
Now, I'm going to change
some of these names just

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.370
to make it a little
easier to explain things.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:31.550
So let's say we'll call
this slider my_slider

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:42.860
and input.my_slider and text,
call it txt_computation.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.898
All right, we'll put
this over here as well.

00:03:45.898 --> 00:03:47.690
So I made some changes
to this application.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:48.482
I'm just going to--

00:03:48.482 --> 00:03:50.120
I can click this
button to rerun it.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.488
Now it looks the same, but
over here, the variables

00:03:53.488 --> 00:03:55.280
should be-- it'll be
a little easier to see

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.200
how things line up.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:05.120
So I've got the slider input,
and the ID of it is my_slider,

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.980
and then I can read it in
this function down here.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.900
So I can say input.my_slider,
and then I invoke that

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:16.010
as a function, and this function
will return the current value

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.470
of the slider.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.540
And then what I'm
doing with that value

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.900
is I'm putting into a string.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:28.640
And this function,
txt_computation, it

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.340
takes this string
and it's sticking it

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.403
into this output_text_verbatim.

00:04:33.403 --> 00:04:34.820
One of the cool
things about Shiny

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.510
is its reactive computing model.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.930
So if I move this
slider, I don't

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:40.910
have to do anything
extra to tell

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:44.120
it to re-execute this function.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.540
It just knows, like, hey,
input.my_slider's changed,

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:51.770
so re-execute this function
every time I do that.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:56.332
All right, now I can do
some other things to this.

00:04:56.332 --> 00:04:57.540
I can change this label here.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:03.780
I can say you input a
value here, and I can--

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:07.380
because this is all running
within the web browser,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.880
Python is running
in the web browser

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.770
and Shiny is running
in the web browser,

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.270
I can just click
this button here

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.090
and re-execute this
application every time

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:15.970
I make changes to it.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.180
So it makes it really easy
to try out new things.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

